oney that's required to make the  movie you want to make in the way that  you want to make it without a screenplay  and that's really where it came down to  was that you know if we could have made  the movie for half a million dollars and  just got in the car then that's one  thing but that's that wasn't the reality  of the resources that they need and they  don't need a lot they're not there  certainly by you know by any means  gratuitous in what they and what they  require for their movies far from it but  it still was enough in this day and age  with this kind of story that there  needed to be more of a blueprint Kenneth  and I think that some extent happens  probably in every film a definitely  every independent film is that it in the  way Lynette and I work as producers it's  all about choices for the filmmaker  and it happened on this film and it's  saying till Annette's point okay well if  you want to do the film in this way and  over this amount of time this is what  it's going to cost it's going to take  for that version of the film this is  what comes with it you know we had the  same experience on Blue Valentine which  we produce together you're Derek  Cianfrance at one point want to take six  years off between the past Blue  Valentine the present and then he wanted  to take six months off and then he  wanted to take three me a boyhood maybe  we should have done that but then he  wanted to take three months off and then  you're trying to convince the bond  company in the financier that it's put  all these people on hold for three  months it's critical to the film and you  know and finally you know he won with  you know what you got one month in the  end and that's probably he wanted from  the start he just said he watched in it  now but it's the same it's the same  thing in every film and those  conversations are tough and they're  never fun when you're trying to give you  know as we work we're trying to give our  filmmakers every tool they can have to  m